5. Downstream Failure Handling

Network failures should not leak low-level exceptions to API consumers. Account Service catches connection-level ResourceAccessException failures and converts them into a controlled dependency error.

Customer Service unavailable
        ↓
ResourceAccessException / downstream 5xx
        ↓
CustomerServiceUnavailableException
        ↓
GlobalExceptionHandler
        ↓
HTTP 503 Service Unavailable

Verified API response:

{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Customer Service is currently unavailable",
  "data": null
}

6. HTTP Timeout Hardening

Explicit timeouts were added to prevent Account Service from waiting indefinitely when Customer Service is unreachable or slow.

HttpClient httpClient = HttpClient.newBuilder()
        .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(3))
        .build();

JdkClientHttpRequestFactory requestFactory =
        new JdkClientHttpRequestFactory(httpClient);

requestFactory.setReadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5));

Timeout

Value

Purpose

Connect

3 seconds

Limits time spent establishing the HTTP connection.

Read

5 seconds

Limits time waiting for the downstream response.

7. Customer Address History Hardening

The Customer Address feature was stabilized as part of the same backend hardening pass. A customer can have address history while maintaining only one current address for each address type.

Only ACTIVE customers can add an address.

An identical current address is rejected.

Replacing a current address expires the previous record and inserts the new record in one transaction.

Address ownership is validated when retrieving an address by ID.

Current-address uniqueness is also protected at the database level.


